1
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:13,570
A network starts with data: many examples, drawn and collected by people.

2
00:00:15,570 --> 00:00:19,097
Each example carries a label, the correct answer, written by a human.

3
00:00:27,670 --> 00:00:33,943
A neuron adds up incoming signals; if the total passes a threshold, it fires.

4
00:00:35,943 --> 00:00:40,216
Weights decide how strongly each signal counts. Thick lines count more.

5
00:00:43,643 --> 00:00:48,395
The guess is compared with the label. The difference is the error, or loss.

6
00:00:50,395 --> 00:00:54,108
The error flows backward, telling every weight how to change a little.

7
00:00:56,395 --> 00:01:00,003
Each small change is a step downhill on the error landscape.

8
00:01:08,195 --> 00:01:12,873
Early layers detect simple strokes; deeper layers combine them into parts.

9
00:01:23,995 --> 00:01:27,916
It recognises a bird it has never seen. That is generalization.

10
00:01:34,795 --> 00:01:39,452
Shown something unlike its data, it can be confidently wrong. People stay in the loop.
